# ThreadSanitizer suppression list for LogSquirl (#347).
#
# Every entry here is a documented false positive or a finding in code TSan
# cannot instrument, not a race in LogSquirl's own logic. See
# docs/adr/0007-tsan-suppresses-onetbb-and-uninstrumented-qt-internals.md for
# how these were investigated and why each is believed safe to suppress.
#
# Applied automatically to every ctest run (cmake/CatchTestDiscoveryRunTest.cmake)
# and documented for standalone binary runs in BUILD.md.
#
# Syntax: https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions

# oneTBB's flow graph (used by the indexing and search pipelines) builds its
# task objects (graph_task and friends) with placement-new over a shared
# small-object pool and downcasts them with static_cast instead of a
# TSan-visible synchronized handoff; its internal spin-locks and atomics that
# actually make this safe are themselves not fully visible to TSan's
# happens-before model in the CPM-vendored build LogSquirl links. This mirrors
# oneTBB's own cmake/suppressions/tsan.suppressions upstream, which suppresses
# the same subsystem for the same reason.
race:tbb::detail::r1::
race:tbb::detail::d1::graph_task
race:tbb::detail::d1::input_node_task_bypass
race:tbb::detail::d1::forward_task_bypass
race:tbb::flow::

# QThreadPoolThread::run() (QtCore, uninstrumented in this build) hands a
# queued runnable from the thread that called QThreadPool::start() to the pool
# thread that executes it, through Qt's own internal mutex/condvar. That
# handoff is a real happens-before edge, but TSan cannot see synchronization
# inside a library that was not built with -fsanitize=thread, so it reports the
# shared_ptr<const RegularExpression> that LogFilteredDataWorker::search()
# captures by value into the queued lambda as a race between the calling
# thread's copy going out of scope and the pool thread's copy being
# constructed/destroyed. Investigated on its own merits (not suppressed
# reflexively): the shared_ptr is copied by value at enqueue time, only ever
# read (never mutated) on the pool thread, and std::shared_ptr's control-block
# refcounting is atomic regardless of who holds the mutex, so concurrent
# copy/destroy of the same shared_ptr from different threads is safe by the
# C++ standard whether or not Qt's mutex is visible to TSan. Conclusion: TSan
# blind spot on uninstrumented Qt internals, not a real race. See the ADR for
# the full reasoning.
race:QThreadPoolThread::run
race:QThreadPool::start
